Simple text encoder so that you get smaller file sizes.



Currently only works with lowercase, and also we can't have character 30 as a
key because that is the escape key. Also want some way of getting just the
right number of substitutions.

For capitalisation, some words are likely to be capitalised by default, such as
names, whereas others will have variation. So the idea should probably be to
alter the escape character based on whether the word needs to be capitalised.
But then is there really any point bothering?

Trying the same program but with a text containing capitalised words just
ignored the capital letters and took symbols of the rest of words, which is
kind of a neat system, but not at all ideal.



Removed the keys in the dictionary, because the keys are just numbers, but the
words are put in order anyway, so you don't really need them.



----


In order to get savings in terms of byte usage, consider the following:

Suppose there is a three letter word in the dictionary, such as 'the'.

In encoded form, the dictionary includes 'and\n', so four bytes, and every
instance of the word looks like '^i' where '^' is the escape code (literally
00011110) and 'i' is whatever the key is for this word, let us suppose it is
the first word in the dictionary, so then the key is literally 00000001.

So, given the file

''' plaintext   - 11 text bytes
the the the
'''

The encoded version is

''' enc.bin     - 12 text bytes
the\n
^i ^i ^i
'''

Indeed, were we to have fewer than three instances of the word, the byte count
would be an expense, not a saving. However:

''' plaintext   - 15 text bytes
the the the the
'''

''' enc.bin     - 15 text bytes
the\n
^i ^i ^i ^i
'''

So then the practice of encoding is really neither here nor there, though does
give us more processing to do later, so might as well not be done. It is clear,
however, that if we add a fifth instance of 'the', then our two files will
contain nineteen and eighteen bytes respectively, and so the savings begin (a
whole byte!).

Note that the precise quantity differs for words of different lenghts:

Given the word 'that':

''' plaintext   - 14 text bytes
that that that
'''

''' enc.bin     - 13 text bytes
that\n
^i ^i ^i
'''

Given the word 'where':

''' plaintext   - 17 text bytes
where where where
'''

''' enc.bin     - 14 text bytes
where\n
^i ^i ^i

And so on. Regard the following table:

Word length | Min. instances for saving | Saving at min.
------------|---------------------------|---------------
1           | n/a                       | n/a
2           | n/a                       | n/a
3           | 5                         | 1
4           | 3                         | 1
5           | "                         | 3
6           | 2                         | 1
7           | "                         | 2
8           | "                         | 3
9           | "                         | 4

Note that these calculations do not include the '^^^' control code that
separates the dictionary from the body text, as this is only ever included
once, and it is suspected that the dictionary will always have multiple words
in it (if not, probably don't bother with the whole bloody scheme).

For words of length one and two, they are never larger than the instances of
'^i', and so no multiple of them will ever create savings with my
implementation.

Note also that for no word will the minimum instances be one, or less, as the
word is always copied out in full in the dictionary, so there will always be
more extra characters than a single instance. This table also shows that
improving my implementation to pick up multi-word symbols would be worthwhile.
